#align(center, text(size: 7pt, tracking: 0.12em)[ABOUT THIS BOOKLET])
#v(0.08in)

Thomas Aquinas (1225-1274) was a Dominican friar who taught in Paris, Naples
and Rome. His _Summa Theologiae_, left unfinished at his death, is a long
sequence of short questions, each broken into articles. The two questions
printed here come from the part of the _Summa_ on temperance, the virtue
that moderates our desires.

That placement is the surprise. Aquinas treats the desire to know the way he
treats the desire for food: it is natural and good, and for that very reason
it needs to be ordered. The virtue that orders it he calls _studiositas_,
studiousness. The vice that disorders it he calls _curiositas_, curiosity.

His "curiosity" is not the healthy wonder we praise in children and
scientists. It is the restless craving to see and to know things that pull
us away from what we ought to be attending to: the spectacle, the novelty,
the neighbor's faults. Studiousness, on the other side, is not only
restraint. It is also the keenness to take up the hard work of learning
what is worth knowing.

The text is eight centuries old. Read it slowly, with a pencil.

#pagebreak()
#align(center, text(size: 7pt, tracking: 0.12em)[HOW TO READ AN ARTICLE])
#v(0.08in)

Every article asks one question, phrased "Whether..." and answered yes or
no. The parts always come in the same order:

*Objections.* Arguments for the answer Aquinas will _reject_. They are
stated fairly, often strongly.

*On the contrary.* A single authority, usually Scripture or a Church Father,
pointing the other way.

*I answer that.* Aquinas's own answer and his reasons. This is the heart of
the article.

*Replies.* A response to each objection in turn, usually granting what is
true in it and showing where it goes wrong.

On a first reading, start with "I answer that," then go back to the
objections and replies.

#v(0.6em)
#text(size: 7.5pt)[
References such as (II-II:166:2 ad 2) point to other places in the _Summa_:
Second Part of the Second Part, Question 166, Article 2, reply to the second
objection. "The Philosopher" is Aristotle.
